For Immediate Release
Explanation of Position on “Joint United Nations Program on HIV/AIDS” Resolution
The United States opposes portions of the text in resolution E/2025/L.25 and dissociates from paragraphs referencing the Sustainable Development Goals [or referencing unhindered access to medicines] or referencing to sexual reproductive health or referring to gender as a broad or undefined concept (PP3, PP7, PP20, OP7, OP9, OP12, OP19 and OP22.
The United States would like to express support for advancing efforts against the global HIV/AIDS epidemic, and the important role the United States has played in coordinating the global response to an epidemic that has claimed many lives, threatening communities, families and the economic growth and the stability of nations most heavily affected by this disease.
The United States has long collaborated with UNAIDS since its creation by ECOSOC and we have appreciated setting clear global agendas to drive down new HIV infections, while ensuring all persons living with HIV have access to treatment. With decades of strong global effort, the UNAIDS 2025 global report shows significant progress in controlling the disease.
It is essential the global health community stays focused on clear lifesaving HIV and prevention and treatment goals, ending the threat of HIV globally.
The United States supports language contained in the report highlighting efforts and actions core to ending AIDS, including the importance of community engagement and the role community-led organizations play in providing HIV prevention and treatment services.
The United States also welcomes language on the UNAIDS’ efforts to advance ending pediatric AIDS, including supporting countries to ensure pregnant women have access to life-saving HIV treatment to prevent the transmission of HIV from mother to child, and children already living with HIV are a priority for all countries.
We want to thank delegations for their constructive cooperation related to the language on the SDGs, including the removal of several references to the 2030 Agenda. The SDGs do not enjoy full consensus and infringe on the sovereignty of Member States. Member States who wish to implement them can do so under their own right. We reject the trend to use the SDGs as a soft global governance tool, and as such, we oppose the remaining references which appear in one preambular and two operative paragraphs (PP3, OP19, and OP22).
While the United States broadly supports the inclusion of text highlighting the need for access to health services for persons living with and at risk of HIV, we do not support references to unhindered access to medicines. Additionally, we elect to avoid listing specific social risk factors or the use of non-consensual terms that historically have sparked lengthy and unhelpful debate such as references to sexual and reproductive health. We categorically reject this language and related terms and disassociate from paragraphs including this language (PP20, OP9).
Similarly, the United States does not support references to gender equality unless explicitly delineating as men and women, including text that supports gender as a broader or undefined concept, which appear in one preambular paragraph and three operative paragraphs (PP7, OP7, OP9, and OP12).
