Jonathan Shrier
Acting Representative to the UN Economic and Social Council
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Vice President Thapa, for chairing this Management Segment for the Council, and we look forward to soon calling you “Mr. President”.
As we reflect on the outcomes of the 34th Commission on Crime Prevention and Criminal Justice, held in May 2025, the United States underscores the urgent need to end the crisis and burden of illegal immigration, including by disrupting alien smuggling networks.
Let the record show that this body has adopted very few resolutions on the matter of alien smuggling. Thanks to the strong support for this U.S. initiative from Commission members, this crime is being elevated as an international priority.
At the 34th Commission, the United States led the successful adoption—by consensus—of a groundbreaking resolution titled “Strengthening International Efforts to Prevent and Combat the Smuggling of Migrants.” The resolution commits countries to take concrete, operational law enforcement actions to disrupt smuggling networks and to hold criminals accountable. Notably, it is the first resolution adopted outside the Security Council to advocate for the sharing of biometric information to detect and prevent criminal and terrorist activities, marking a significant advancement in international efforts to combat alien smuggling.
We have brought attention to the obvious: alien smuggling networks for far too long have operated with impunity, and countries need to do more to disrupt them.
This resolution is more than a statement of intent—it is a decisive call to action on the 25th anniversary of the UN Convention against Transnational Organized Crime (UNTOC) Protocol against Migrant Smuggling that underscores the need for improved implementation. We urge all countries to act swiftly and effectively, translating the resolution’s promise into meaningful, real-world impact.
We look forward to the adoption of the report of the 34th Crime Commission, and we expect to hear countries’ progress in developing national strategies and action plans to implement the UNTOC Protocol at the 35th Commission, consistent with paragraph 13 of this resolution.
A nation cannot exist without borders. Each nation has a responsibility to preserve national sovereignty. Nations that refuse to combat alien smuggling networks within their borders infringe upon the sovereignty of other nations.
Thank you, Mr. President.
