Jonathan Shrier
Acting Representative to the UN Economic and Social Council
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. The United States thanks Bangladesh for its leadership and efforts in facilitating the process on this resolution. The United States strongly supports prevention of conflict and peacebuilding efforts.
However, this resolution contains positions that the United States cannot support.
First, as we have expressed previously, the United States will no longer reaffirm the 2030 Agenda as a matter of course.
Second, the resolution uses the term “gender” rather than recognizing the sexes. It is U.S. policy to recognize two sexes, male and female, and not to promote gender ideology.
Third, the resolution recalls the Paris Agreement, with respect to which the United States has notified its withdrawal, consistent with Executive Order 14162 on “Putting America First in International Environmental Agreements.” The resolution also recalls the Sevilla Commitment, with respect to which the United States is not a party.
Furthermore, this resolution also exemplifies the problematic trend of excessively lengthy resolutions.
For these reasons, therefore, the United States will vote no on resolution A/79/L.111.
Thank you.
