Jonathan Shrier
Acting Representative to the UN Economic and Social Council
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President, and let me express appreciation for the extensive work of the cofacilitators, the distinguished PRs of Czechia and St. Vincent and the Grenadines.
As we have stated, the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) advance a program of soft global governance that is inconsistent with national sovereignty and adverse to the rights and interests of Americans.
The United States therefore rejects the HLPF Ministerial Declaration.
The United States has always supported strong, broad-based economic growth, an unwavering commitment to individual rights, and responsible environmental protections at home and abroad.
These are the values we promote, and we will continue to lead by example, taking cues from the generosity of the American people and the innovation and standards of our private sector.
In the UN, the United States is guided by our moral and civic duty to protect the interests of U.S. citizens. Our position on the 2030 Agenda is a reflection of that commitment.
The HLPF, and UN system writ large, use the SDGs to promulgate divisive cultural causes. The outrageous DEI initiative termed “multiple and intersecting forms of discrimination” and the radical abortion agenda reflexively advanced by the UN and certain Member States has no business in this ministerial outcome.
Member states share common goals to deliver prosperity, transparency, and accountability to their citizens.
However, the fundamental principle of national sovereignty and national responsibility for sustainable development has been lost.
Over the last 10 years, Member States and UN institutions have pursued an ever-widening mandate to achieve sustainable development in an attempt to turn a set of aspirational, voluntary goals into a mandate to finance other countries’ development.
They have used the SDGs to attempt to rewrite the rules of the multilateral system at the expense of U.S. interests and those of our allies. The United States will not accept such provisions, including any provisions that would exceed the UN’s authority.
The international financial institutions have independent mandates and authorities, and we do not support attempts in the General Assembly or elsewhere in the UN system to dictate their priorities or activities.
The 2030 Agenda cannot dictate general trade policy to Member States, or constrain the application of legal and legitimate sanctions on malign actors.
Issues related to international intellectual property law are outside of the scope of the HLPF mandate. The United States rejects attempts by Member States to curb well-respected protections to enrich their own economies at the expense of U.S. companies and citizens.
The United States rejects collectivist catchphrases like “right to development” that are used to undermine the rights of the individual and which some governments use to obfuscate human rights abuses against their own people.
We reject both the authority and competence of the HLPF to establish or promote debt review mechanisms or reform existing debt treatment frameworks. We reject mechanisms that would undermine the rights of creditors, including the U.S. government and private creditors with debts contracted under U.S. law, to seek payment on the amounts they are owed. In cases of debt distress, debt treatments must remain voluntary agreements between creditors and debtors and should be conducted in a manner that does not undermine the sound functioning of international capital markets.
We reject the idea that national success is contingent upon increased donor funding. We reject the attempts by some nations to justify their international political agendas as furthering the SDGs.
Instead, countries should prioritize national policies that foster the enabling environment necessary for development. This includes taking action against the criminal groups and cartels that are killing our citizens and flooding the streets with illegal drugs.
Consistent with countries’ national sovereignty and national responsibility for sustainable development, we emphasize the centrality of the mobilization and effective use of domestic resources and strong partnerships with the private sector to encourage private investment.
The United States is committed to ensuring that our resources, including those related to international assistance, are used effectively and efficiently. Foreign assistance must drive development outcomes and diminish the need for assistance in the long run.
We will not agree to any measures that impede national sovereignty or contravene the plain language of our March 4 Explanation of Vote concerning the 2030 Agenda.
Thank you.
