Jonathan Shrier
Acting Representative to the UN Economic and Social Council
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President.
We also want to thank Germany for their work on Afghanistan and this resolution, A/79/L.100.; however, the United States must vote no on the resolution. Nearly four years following the Taliban takeover, we continue the same conversations and engage with the same so-called Taliban officials about improving the situation in Afghanistan without demanding results from them. In fact, we have rewarded the Taliban’s failure with more engagement and more resources. This resolution is more of the same.
In the areas of counterterrorism, counter-narcotics, and security writ large, it is in our mutual interests to ensure that Afghanistan never again harbors terrorists and is a net contributor to regional stability and cooperation rather than a detractor. The Taliban must cease their hostage diplomacy once and for all.
For decades, the United States sacrificed American lives and trillions of dollars to achieve these mutually shared goals, and to support the people of Afghanistan. Many of the countries represented in this assembly did the same.
Mr. President, it is the position of the United States that we cannot continue propping up the Taliban while simultaneously demanding they meet their international commitments and respect Afghanistan’s international obligations. These stand in direct contradiction to each other. The existing approach to humanitarian assistance for the Afghan people is not sustainable and will continue to fail to yield the intended results.
With regard to international cooperation and the UN-led Doha process, it too has yielded few sustainable results. The United States is deeply skeptical about the Taliban’s willingness to participate in good faith. When they are willing to engage, it is only on their terms and to the detriment of our interests. Their actions have left us unconvinced they will ever offer real concessions or pursue policies in accordance with the expectations of the international community.
We are also concerned about the resolution’s appreciation for the Islamic Republic of Iran, a government that is the world’s leading sponsor of terrorism, that continues to execute Afghans at an alarming rate without due process, and that has forcibly conscripted Afghans into militias that destabilize the Middle East.
Mr. President, the United States calls upon the Taliban to stop behaving as an insurgency. Accordingly, we demand the Taliban release all detained Americans and end their hostage diplomacy.
Change in Afghanistan must come from within. For decades, we shouldered the burden of supporting the Afghan people with time, money, and, most important, American lives. It is due time for the Taliban to step up. The United States will no longer enable their heinous behavior.
Thank you.
