Jonathan Shrier
Acting Representative to the UN Economic and Social Council
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr President.
Respect for state sovereignty is a foundational principle of international peace and stability. Yet this principle has been steadily eroded in favor of vague collective responsibilities and expanding frameworks of global governance.
The Responsibility to Protect (R2P) is a case in point and a dangerous concept. By encouraging interventions in conflicts that often lie beyond a state’s strategic interests, R2P opens the door to selective, politicized action under the guise of humanitarian concern—an approach that is ultimately destabilizing and threatens the very international order it claims to uphold.
The United States will always act in accordance with our national interest and will not subordinate our sovereignty to shifting international norms, and we encourage others to do the same.
The 2005 World Summit Outcome is a political commitment. It is not legally binding and, moreover, as affirmed in Paragraph 138, the responsibility is for each individual State to protect its populations from genocide, war crimes, ethnic cleansing, and crimes against humanity.
Some Member States must do much more to address the risks that lead to atrocities and to put an end to senseless conflicts. Let me cite three examples.
First, China’s longstanding genocide and crimes against humanity committed against Uyghurs show it blatantly violates its human rights obligations.
The United States is committed to holding to account those responsible and to combatting China’s efforts to pressure other governments to forcibly return Uyghurs and other individuals to China, where they are subject to torture and enforced disappearances.
We call on Chinese authorities to cease their abuses in Xinjiang, including arbitrary imprisonment, torture, forced labor, and the imposition of draconian restrictions on fundamental freedoms.
Second, concerning Myanmar, UN experts have reported that China continues to provide arms and military assistance to the Myanmar military regime, a regime whose airstrikes continue to kill civilians and destroy schools, medical facilities, and houses of worship.
We condemn in the strongest terms the Myanmar regime’s violence and ongoing human rights abuses since the 2021 military coup. This has fueled conflicts that have led to more than 3.5 million internally displaced people. We appreciate UN Special Envoy Julie Bishop’s unrelenting efforts to resolve the crisis in Myanmar.
Third, the United States is deeply concerned about the escalating conflict in Sudan, including reported attacks on civilians and drone attacks reportedly by the Rapid Support Forces (RSF).
This is simply unacceptable. We call for an immediate end to the violence.
By many measures, the conflict in Sudan is the worst humanitarian crisis in the world today. It is imperative that the RSF and the SAF allow unhindered humanitarian access and protect civilians, including by allowing the safe passage of civilians fleeing violence.
In closing, the United States will continue to advance the cause of respect for human life, an end to senseless conflicts, and a restoration of peace. We urge all Member States to do the same.
Thank you.
